# What leaves this machine on every deploy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. There wasn't one. `fly deploy` tars the whole working
# directory and uploads it to Fly's remote builder as build context — every
# deploy, regardless of what the Dockerfile actually COPYs. The Dockerfile is
# narrow (package.json, package-lock.json, tsconfig.json, src), so none of this
# ever reached the image; but the CONTEXT is a separate thing from the image,
# and the context was everything: `.env`, `.internal-marker`, `.stats-token`,
# and `data/` with the full settlement ledger and every payer address in it.
#
# Fly already holds those secrets — they are set as Fly secrets — so this was
# poor hygiene rather than a breach, and it is not being escalated as one. But
# "our credentials and our customer ledger leave the machine every ten minutes"
# is not a sentence anyone had decided to be true. Found by Security while
# arguing about where private docs should live; the mechanism turned out to
# matter more than the argument did.
#
# DENY BY DEFAULT, then re-include. The allowlist is the point: a new secret
# file dropped in the repo root is excluded because it was never named, rather
# than included because nobody remembered to name it. Every deny-list version of
# this file is one forgotten entry away from the thing it exists to prevent —
# the same reason the buyer-waiting eviction refuses new ids instead of guessing.
*

# Exactly what the Dockerfile builds from, and nothing else.
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!tsconfig.json
!src
!src/**

# Re-exclude anything that could hide under src.
src/**/*.env
src/**/.env*
